# LaTeX Engineering

Distilled from: X-LANCE Lab pre-print template (xlance.cls v0.2.4-20260429), Overleaf "Management in a large project", thetapad.com large LaTeX projects, dmsussman.org LaTeX collaboration guide.

## Document class (current: xlance.cls)

- Document class is `\documentclass[opendfm]{xlance/xlance}`. The class lives under `xlance/` together with `IEEEtran2.bst`, `venues.bib` (X-LANCE-managed venue strings) and `assets/logos/`. Do not edit files under `xlance/` -- treat the directory as a vendored library; if you need a tweak, override it in `main.tex` preamble.
- Theme is OpenDFM (blue-purple). To switch back to X-LANCE red, drop the `[opendfm]` option.
- The class already loads, in this order: `geometry, microtype, placeins, hyphenat, setspace, parskip, babel, etoolbox, fontenc, tgpagella, mathpazo, inconsolata, amsmath, amssymb, mathtools, fancyhdr, graphicx, subcaption, float, booktabs, nicematrix, multirow, bm, longtable, tabularx, array, adjustbox, xspace, enumitem, tcolorbox, xcolor[table,svgnames], listings, hyperref, cleveref, natbib`. Do **not** re-`\usepackage` any of these in `main.tex` -- it triggers Option clash.
- The class redefines `\title`, `\author[N]{...}`, `\affiliation[N]{...}`, `\contribution{...}`, `\abstract{...}`, `\metadatainfo{...}`, `\publishdate`, `\revisedate`, `\maketitle`. `\equalcontribution{Name}` and `\correspondence{email}{Name}` are protected commands meant to be wrapped around an `\author{}` argument.
- `\abstract{...}` is a **command, not an environment**. The body lives in `sections/00_abstract.tex` as plain prose; `main.tex` consumes it via `\abstract{\input{sections/00_abstract}}` in the preamble.
- The first page header logos are suppressed in `main.tex` by overriding `\fancypagestyle{firstpage}` (after the class is loaded). Do not undo this unless the team formally adopts X-LANCE / OpenDFM branding.
- The first page after `\maketitle` is reserved for a teaser figure (currently `figures/architecture.pdf` rendered as `\Cref{fig:teaser}`) followed by `\tableofcontents`. Keep section-level architecture references via `\Cref{fig:teaser}` rather than re-embedding the figure inside `sections/04_model.tex`.

## File layout

- `main.tex` is orchestration only: documentclass, preamble overrides, title/author/abstract, `\input{sections/...}`, bibliography, appendix. No prose body in `main.tex`.
- `sections/XX_name.tex` -- one section per file, prefixed by 2-digit order.
- `figures/<name>.tex` -- TikZ source. Build with `latexmk -pdf` from project root; the resulting `figures/<name>.pdf` is checked in.
- `references.bib` -- our own bibliography. Combined with `xlance/venues.bib` at compile time via `\bibliography{xlance/venues,references}`.
- `xlance/` -- vendored template, treat as read-only.
- Tables that exceed ~30 rows or are script-generated: separate file under `tables/`, `\input` from the relevant section.

## When editing sections

- Prefer `\input{sections/XX_name}` over `\include{}`.
- Use `\includeonly{sections/05_training}` in `main.tex` only as a temporary local edit when iterating fast on one section. Remove before commit.
- Cross-reference: always use `\label{type:name}` with one of these prefixes -- `sec:`, `subsec:`, `fig:`, `tab:`, `eq:`, `app:`, `alg:`. Reference with `\Cref` (cleveref capitalised; produces "Section 4" / "Figure 1" automatically).
- Math: define recurring symbols once in `main.tex` preamble with `\newcommand`. Examples already defined: `\wer`, `\cer`, `\mer`, `\hotwerN`. Do not redefine in sections.
- Code listings: use one of the four styles shipped by xlance.cls -- `python`, `json`, `bash`, `plain`. The `plain` style is the project's default for prompt examples. For colored prompt boxes use the `promptbox` tcolorbox environment from xlance.cls; for note callouts use `notebox`.

## Bibliography rules (IEEE numeric)

- Citation format is IEEE numeric `[1]` via `natbib + xlance/IEEEtran2.bst` with `\setcitestyle{numbers}`.
- Use `\cite{key}` for all citations (parenthetical and inline). The numeric back-end ignores the `\citep / \citet` distinction. Multi-cites: `\cite{a, b, c}` not `\cite{a}\cite{b}`.
- BibTeX key format: `<lastname><year><firstcontentword>`, lowercase, no punctuation. Example: `gulati2020conformer`, `radford2023whisper`. Existing keys must not be renamed (cross-section refs would break silently).
- Every new cite must include: author (full names with `and`), year, title, booktitle / journal, volume / number / pages where applicable. Prefer the published-venue entry over arXiv when both exist; if both are needed, keep the published one and omit the other.
- The `xlance/venues.bib` file is X-LANCE-managed conference / journal name strings. We do not add custom keys there; put new entries in `references.bib` and rely on `xlance/venues.bib` only when its `@string` macros help canonicalise venue names.
- After adding a cite, run a build right away to make sure `bibtex` resolves it.

## Numbers, units, and typography

- Thousand separator inside math: `10{,}000` (the `{}` keeps the comma upright).
- Numbers and units always glued with a thin space: `960\,h`, `16\,kHz`, `7\,B`, `5e-7` is fine in tables.
- Percent sign: `5\%` (escape the `%`).
- Em-dash for parenthetical breaks: `---` (LaTeX renders as one character). Do not use a hyphen.
- Quotes: ``  `single' `` and `` ``double'' ``. Never `"..."`.
- Algorithm-style code or prompts: use `lstlisting` with `style=plain` (or `python` / `json` / `bash` for typed code). Do not `\verb|...|` for multi-line content.
- Convenience commands shipped by xlance.cls: `\eg`, `\ie`. Use them for "e.g." and "i.e." where appropriate (renders italic with the trailing comma + thin space already attached).

## Compile workflow

- Default: `PATH=/Library/TeX/texbin:$PATH latexmk -pdf -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex`
- After every substantive content edit, run `latexmk` and confirm exit code 0 before reporting completion.
- If a fresh package is needed: `sudo tlmgr install <pkg>`. Update the "Required tlmgr packages" note in `README.md` so a fresh checkout reproduces.
- Do not commit `main.pdf` (`.gitignore` already enforces). Do commit `figures/*.pdf` for reproducibility.

## What never to do

- Do not silently change the document class, theme option (`opendfm` / default), or geometry; these affect reviewer page count and headers.
- Do not re-`\usepackage` anything that xlance.cls already loads (see the list above) -- it produces an `Option clash` error.
- Do not introduce a package whose macros conflict with xlance.cls's redefinitions of `\section`, `\maketitle`, `\abstract`, `\title`, `\author`, `\affiliation`. If unsure, build first and inspect the log.
- Do not use Unicode characters in source where a LaTeX command exists (use `\alpha` not `α`, `\textendash` not `--` if you specifically want an en-dash).
- Do not edit files inside `xlance/`. Override behaviour in `main.tex` preamble (e.g. our `\fancypagestyle{firstpage}` redefinition for logo suppression).
